hello and welcome to the daily climate  show coming up  don't lose hope and don't lose focus the  message from the un as diplomats gather  in germany to review progress on climate  action  sales of electric vehicles rockets have  made a wider slump in the uk's car  industry  and as a new law comes into effect  banning the trade of ivory in the uk  campaigners fear it might be too late  [Music]  hello and welcome to the daily climate  show here on sky news where we track the  changes happening to the world look at  the progress that is being made to deal  with those changes and of course hear  from the people who are coming up with  the solutions  now the war in ukraine and energy  security will be weighing on the minds  of international climate diplomats as  they gather in bond today to assess  progress  on international climate action now cop  26 in glasgow feels like a long time ago  now doesn't it but countries then we're  united on the need for quicker action by  cutting carbon emissions well today the  world is distracted and many see climate  action stalling  in her opening speech the u.n climate  chief patricia espinosa emphasized the  need for much faster progress  we need decisions and actions now and it  is incumbent on all nations to make  progress here in bonn in the coming two  weeks  and we must understand that climate  change is moving  exponentially  we can no longer afford to make  just incremental progress  we must move these negotiations along  more quickly  the world expects it  while our europe correspondent siobhan  robbins joins me now and siobhan what  progress or lack thereof has there been  since glasgow  well if we cast our minds back to  november last year you'll remember some  of those pledges from glasgow so moving  away from coal cutting emissions halting  deforestation helping some of the  poorest countries to deal with the  impacts of climate change and frankly  some of the progress over the last six  seven months on some of those topics has  been pretty depressing so for example in  terms of deforestation we've actually  seen record levels of deforestation in  the amazon  also in terms of helping some of those  poorest countries we're still not  hitting the target for the amount of  money to be given to those countries  also coal because of the problems with  ukraine because of the problems with  energy security some countries are now  clinging on to coal or even investing in  it so it hasn't been a very pretty  picture in that sense but there have  been a few glimmers of hope so for  example china has seen the longest  sustained  sustained drop in co2 emissions and  that's mainly down to a drop in output  in cement factories and steel however  they are still investing a lot in gas  and coal so we need to see if that  emissions drop continues  um so siobhan what are the main aims  then of this meeting in bonn  so the meeting will last for the next  two weeks and essentially what this is  is the kind of like looking back to  glasgow and saying what do we need to do  but then also looking forward to cop 27  which will take place in egypt and  saying what can we then build on so  they're going to be looking at things  like getting cash for poorer countries  uh how to cut emissions uh how to move  away from coal things like that it's  basically implementation and policy  and we have heard from the u.n climate  chief today who was saying that they  can't afford to dordle that climate  change has set its own pace and that  they have to react to it but also  issuing a rallying cry saying despite  the other crises facing the world they  have to focus on climate change  shivan thank you she won robins there in  berlin  well let's get some of today's other  climate news now in australia's prime  minister has offered indonesia a  115 million pound climate partnership as  an attempt to deepen the diplomatic  relationship between the two countries  now starting their meeting by riding on  bamboo bicycles around the presidential  palace anthony albanese and president  joko widodo discuss regional cooperation  and security now the new australian  leader also offered assistance for  indonesia's new green high-tech capital  which is due to be relocated from  jakarta to the island of borneo  the rapid growth in sales of electric  vehicles was the only bright spot in  rather gloomy news for the uk's car  industry today  car sales overall dropped by nearly 21  last month the second weakest may in  three decades is after 2020 when  showrooms were of course closed because  of the lockdown  but electric vehicles sales jumped by  nearly 18  and so the faster the growth in  battery-powered vehicles then of course  the faster emissions from the transport  sector will drop  and the u.n secretary general has called  for a new way for the world to measure  success  in an appeal for more sustainable  economic growth antonio guterres tweeted  when we destroy forests over fish or  burn coal  then we are increasing gross domestic  products it's time to change an  accountant system he continued that  counts the destruction of the planets as  if it was the products of richness  the new law banning the trade of ivory  throughout the uk comes into effect  today elephant population  have long been in decline due to habitat  loss and of course hunting for their  tasks so the new law has been welcomed  by conservationists however an  investigation by international wildlife  charity born free has uncovered evidence  that large volumes of ivory are still  being sold online  threatening the survival of wild  elephants fraser malt has this report  protecting the earth's largest land  mammal is a battle fort on many fronts  the ban on ivory sales is just one of  them and from today it'll be illegal for  ivory to be sold to in or from the uk  one of the biggest threats to elephants  is the trade in ivory the illegal trade  in ivory and by having no legal markets  you were closing doors for people to  launder illegal ivory through those  marketplaces so you are giving them a  bit of a reprieve unsurprisingly the  internet has become a prime resource for  the ivory dealers  to avoid ivory bans already in place on  some internet marketplaces unscrupulous  dealers often describe their items as  being made of bone horn or antler or  from artificial materials like bakelite  resin or ivorine  figures from the bourne free foundation  suggest the online market for ivory in  the uk sees almost 2 000 listings a  month worth over a million pounds  although not always easily identified by  far the most common source of the ivory  being sold on the internet is from  elephants  but the act doesn't prohibit selling  elephant ivory alternatives from other  living animals with tusks or teeth or  even from extinct species like the  mammoth which raises concerns that the  covert selling of elephant ivory could  increase along with the threat of other  animals being exploited  this law is a significant improvement on  the  previous ivory regulations and  we welcome it but of course we have some  reservations about not including  non-elephant ivory species for example  by only banning elephant ivory ivory  from other animals such as walruses and  hippos will increase in demand  conservationists question why anyone  would want to own items like these when  their very existence increases the  threat to one of the world's most  intelligent and iconic creatures  fraser moored sky news  and that's it from me for now thanks for  watching see you next time  [Music]
 

